#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 960.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097447} :activity/id #uuid "cb0bf575-ae5d-5289-83a0-5fe4af2c7572" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048862 :topic/id #uuid "c7162eba-3656-5b62-8b28-391c01075d02" :topic/math-academy-id 1086} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097447 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097910 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we want to approximate the area $\\mathcal A$ bound by the curve $y= x^2+1$ and the lines $x=0,$ $x=1,$ and the $x$-axis. We can do this using a left Riemann sum with $n=4$ subintervals, as shown below:\n\n![](images/1b/1b25a722d5527db77cd7dd5fb581c0ffed9b9324913d390e2633211360b6a8ab.png)\n\nA better approximation can be found by taking more subintervals. For instance, taking $n=8$ subintervals gives:\n\n![](images/17/17f6c8e458b2e6fbd887728cbbcc7cb64a670598256f76cad69d9e5a9c3cf7b5.png)\n\nAs we continue to increase $n,$ our approximation gets better and better. In fact, we can get an *exact* value for the area by taking the limit of the Riemann sum as $n\\to\\infty.$\n\nLet's write down that limit. First, we need to express the left Riemann sum using sigma notation. Using $n$ subintervals between the endpoints $a=0$ and $b=1,$ we express\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})Δx \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f(0+k\\cdot Δx)Δx \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f(k\\cdot Δx)Δx \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}({(k\\cdot Δx)}^{2}+1)Δx \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}({k}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}+1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}(\\frac{{k}^{2}}{{n}^{2}}+1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the above, we used the fact that $x_k = 0 + k\\Delta x = k\\Delta x$ and $\\Delta x = \\dfrac{1 - 0}{n} = \\dfrac 1 n,$ as we've seen before.\n\nThe exact area is found by computing the limit as $n\\to\\infty.$ So instead of using an approximation symbol $(\\approx),$ we can now use an equality symbol and write\n\n$$\nA = \\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}\\left(\\dfrac{k^2}{n^2} + 1\\right)\\cdot \\dfrac{1}{n}.\n$$\n\nThe formula above is a bit clunky, so instead of writing it using limits and sigma notation, we introduce a new notation called the **definite integral**, defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} = \\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1}\\left(\\dfrac{k^2}{n^2} + 1\\right)\\cdot \\dfrac{1}{n}= \\int_0^1 (x^2 + 1)\\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our example, \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int_0^1 (x^2+1)\\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\ncan be interpreted as the exact area under the graph of $y = f(x) = x^2+1$ between $x=0,$ $x=1,$ and the $x$-axis." :tutorial/id #uuid "10c42cfd-895e-5844-b943-efc5be835821" :tutorial/math-academy-id 759 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "dbe8a28f-ae9c-5fce-a185-e8029413c272" :step/math-academy-id 631552 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097449}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097448 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096630 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096431 :question/id #uuid "e375ba82-b677-5e41-a81f-cea18470ab4a" :question/math-academy-id "e-405"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "89902023-09e6-50cd-b08a-9d3e82d826c9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050517}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097210} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097211} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233553} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233959} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235420} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250126} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297281} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297288} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319069} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504624} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504695}] :knowledge-point/title "Writing the Area Bounded by a Curve as a Definite Integral"} :step/id #uuid "9c8a3595-093e-5ea9-b728-e20ce01d63e5" :step/math-academy-id 631554 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097450}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097449 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097911 :tutorial/content "In general, for a function $f(x)$ defined on $[a,b],$ the **definite integral** of $f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be defined using the limit of a left  Riemann sum as\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b f(x)\\textrm{d}x = \\lim_{n\\rightarrow\\infty}\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1} f(x_k)\\Delta x,\n$$\n\n    \n\nwhere $\\Delta x = \\dfrac{b-a}{n}$ and $x_k = a + k\\Delta x.$\n\nThe limit $x=a$ is referred to as the **lower limit**, whereas $x=b$ is called the **upper limit**." :tutorial/id #uuid "9ef3b0cc-ce87-5a51-9a34-0e45b0a342d5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 761 :tutorial/title "General Notation"} :step/id #uuid "b767cb65-6bfb-54c1-b5e9-034fb71e87da" :step/math-academy-id 631553 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097448}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097450 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096631 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096432 :question/id #uuid "cd5b3d07-472e-50cc-b584-569feb90f927" :question/math-academy-id "e-406"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cdc82e49-5df6-5096-a8fd-e00017fb1e3a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048988}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097264} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097268} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233193} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233960} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234107} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234340} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235476} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250128} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250129} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319070} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332671} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341918} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386717} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393250} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393257} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504632} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504672}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Definite Integral as the Limit of a Left Riemann Sum"} :step/id #uuid "1ed0244b-1489-5e87-9ec9-dd2f6566ad85" :step/math-academy-id 631557 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097451}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097451 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096632 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096433 :question/id #uuid "f7aaebcf-0a91-510e-8f09-513774affd6b" :question/math-academy-id "e-407"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4f663b0b-9b5e-512c-92bb-0ea84d5baac5" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097262} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097270} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233800} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234108} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234342} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250137} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319071} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319077} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319081} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319082} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332681} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186366158} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396620} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186396680} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504619} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504654} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504658}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Limit of a Left Riemann Sum as a Definite Integral"} :step/id #uuid "dfbce95d-2f31-5252-bde7-c60a98b5fb17" :step/math-academy-id 631560 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097452}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097452 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097914 :tutorial/content "There's nothing special about using a left Riemann sum to define a definite integral. A definite integral can also be defined as the limit of a right Riemann sum,\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b f(x)\\,\\textrm d x = \\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\sum_{k=1}^n f(x_k)\\cdot\\Delta x,\n$$\n\nwhere $x_k = a + k\\Delta x$ and $\\Delta x =  \\dfrac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nNothing has changed, except that the index starts from $k=1$ instead of $k=0$, and goes up to $k=n.$ \n\n**Note:** In the limit as $n \\to \\infty,$ the left and right Riemann sums give *the exact same result*!" :tutorial/id #uuid "c1ade2ee-0ed4-5dad-b2fc-a3a517dd3f1d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 764 :tutorial/title "Using a Right Riemann Sum"} :step/id #uuid "1cf484cc-b168-5f89-a236-0169328a1312" :step/math-academy-id 631563 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097453}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097453 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096633 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096434 :question/id #uuid "b21e2c30-fe7d-5f6b-ade2-7324be8d273a" :question/math-academy-id "e-408"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29b136d3-69bc-51ef-a262-dbee8af377d2" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097263} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097269} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233196} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233198} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233961} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234344} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297432} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319085} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332672} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341919} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186365571} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386730} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393264} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393271} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504609} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504685} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504688} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504700}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Definite Integral as the Limit of a Right Riemann Sum"} :step/id #uuid "0e12f8ef-911e-5e4b-85e0-2c32f12ef0b6" :step/math-academy-id 631564}] :activity/title "Defining Definite Integrals Using Left and Right Riemann Sums" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186096875}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088823} :activity/id #uuid "bb9db01e-936b-52c3-b219-e1950b5ae188" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048904 :topic/id #uuid "cadfbf13-0689-5ad0-86fa-0607bad5c76c" :topic/math-academy-id 812} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088823 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088899 :tutorial/content "The instantaneous rate of change of a function $f(x)$ at the point $x=a$, denoted $f'(a)$, is given by the limit\n\n$$\nf'(a) = \\lim_{h\\to 0}\\dfrac{f(a+h) - f(a)}{h}\n$$\n\nThis formula works for a specific point $x=a$. If we want a method that works for a *general* point $x$, then we substitute $a=x$ in the formula and get\n\n$$\nf'(x) = \\lim_{h\\to 0}\\dfrac{f(x+h) - f(x)}{h}\n$$\n\nThe function $f'(x)$ is called the **derivative of $f(x)$ with respect to $x$**. It gives the slope of the tangent line to the curve $y=f(x)$ at a general point with coordinates $(x,f(x))$." :tutorial/id #uuid "098fd6b0-306b-544a-9e7f-b301ff48f525" :tutorial/math-academy-id 327 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "454777cc-cdc5-5db8-b536-5b674f1199d6" :step/math-academy-id 605 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088824}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088824 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087880 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087431 :question/id #uuid "36199fc1-505f-5179-9eeb-bec0534bab5f" :question/math-academy-id "e-128"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e905212-8796-5742-a83e-892fdf66eced" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049362}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088191} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088192}] :knowledge-point/title "Constructing an Expression that Represents the Derivative of a Function"} :step/id #uuid "37cc5660-640c-5836-92be-6ff27138fdcb" :step/math-academy-id 606 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088827}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088827 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088900 :tutorial/content "For a given curve $y=f(x)$, the derivative can be represented using several different notations. They are\n\n$$\nf'(x),\\qquad y'(x), \\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}x}, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nIn words, the term $y'(x)$ is said like\n\n- \"$y$ prime of $x$,\" or\n- \"$y$ prime,\" or sometimes\n- \"$y$ dash.\"\n\nThe term\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nis said like \"dee $y$ by dee $x$.\"\n\nThe important thing to remember is that they all represent the same thing, the derivative." :tutorial/id #uuid "cda9f5ac-1ad1-50ce-8bc4-ee5f3be5fd92" :tutorial/math-academy-id 329 :tutorial/title "Alternative Definitions For the Derivative"} :step/id #uuid "1b806b59-9344-54a4-8988-8cb76d4286c3" :step/math-academy-id 609 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088828}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088828 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087881 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087432 :question/id #uuid "7fb64010-dc03-5db5-b5d1-563d2fe44b6d" :question/math-academy-id "e-130"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9c6f3c71-d11b-59af-bad6-5f5c0ff6634f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088189} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088190} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233549} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235416}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Function"} :step/id #uuid "2c77b3ab-7255-54eb-92d4-2ad06b30a0cd" :step/math-academy-id 613}] :activity/title "Defining the Derivative Using Derivative Notation" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186087898}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097443} :activity/id #uuid "1ca5c8c5-8ab4-5833-8f2c-b4b4eb385b25" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048988 :topic/id #uuid "d152b82b-357d-5dd0-8cd0-d9f05720adc2" :topic/math-academy-id 1042} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097443 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097908 :tutorial/content "Remember that the formula for the left Riemann sum for $y=f(x)$ with a regular step size over the interval $[a,b]$ is\n\n$$\n({\\color{red}{f(x_0)}} + {\\color{red}{f(x_1)}} + \\ldots + {\\color{red}{f(x_{n-1})}}) \\cdot {\\color{blue}{\\Delta x}},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of rectangles, $x_0=a$ and $x_{n}=b$ are the endpoints of the interval, and the step size is $\\Delta x = \\dfrac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nThe formula above contains a lot of addition signs. We can simplify this by writing the sum in the **sigma notation**:\n\n$$\n\\sum_{k=0}^{n-1} {\\color{red}f(x_k)}\\,{\\color{blue}\\Delta x},\n$$\n\nwhere $x_k = a+ k\\Delta x$  for  $k=0,1,2,\\ldots ,n-1.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "24aaf571-5125-51e2-baea-075b51bb9415" :tutorial/math-academy-id 751 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "c6c39a44-fae6-59e4-a645-dca1c21bd8e7" :step/math-academy-id 1444 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097444}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097444 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096628 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096428 :question/id #uuid "87747005-ce77-5ec6-9f44-af074633aa98" :question/math-academy-id "e-401"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "62c34646-f7cf-5ffd-b957-4151bdbb5e44" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048363} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048922} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049317}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097266} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097271} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233956} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233957} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233958} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234110} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234334} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234338} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234339} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297427} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297428} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317568}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Left Riemann Sum in Sigma Notation"} :step/id #uuid "b882a5bd-1c56-5f3d-8a4f-5365c326314b" :step/math-academy-id 1445 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097445}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097445 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097909 :tutorial/content "Recall that the formula for the right Riemann sum for $y=f(x)$ over the interval $[a,b]$ is\n\n$$\n({\\color{red}f(x_1)} + {\\color{red}f(x_2)}  + \\ldots + {\\color{red}f(x_n)}) \\cdot {\\color{blue}\\Delta x}.\n$$\n\nThis right Riemann sum in sigma notation is\n\n$$\n\\sum_{k=1}^{n} {\\color{red}f(x_k)}{\\color{blue}\\Delta x},\n$$\n\nwhere $x_k=a+k\\Delta x$  and $\\Delta x = \\dfrac{b-a}{n}.$\n\nNotice that this is very similar to the left Riemann sum. The only differences are that the counter starts at $k=1$ instead of $k=0,$ and the counter ends at $k=n$ instead of $k=n-1.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "44b80895-643f-5bed-a338-f7b8d2b8253c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 753 :tutorial/title "Right Riemann Sums in Sigma Notation"} :step/id #uuid "9d832977-00e5-5dd1-b7cb-a3a6c5ce6f37" :step/math-academy-id 1448 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097446}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097446 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096629 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096429 :question/id #uuid "af666c8f-dfb7-548e-a8f1-1922d4c2fdc5" :question/math-academy-id "e-402"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8d01d6ed-1c25-5170-a1ad-7a4fa0103db7" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049386}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097265} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097267} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233230} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233552} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233793} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234109} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234341} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234343} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234345} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235419} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317569} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318960} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318961} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341921}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Right Riemann Sum in Sigma Notation"} :step/id #uuid "6328686b-7515-5dd5-93ff-47fe41a2f7e3" :step/math-academy-id 1449}] :activity/title "Left and Right Riemann Sums in Sigma Notation" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186096850}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065250} :activity/id #uuid "073f76c5-241e-56ac-b230-c4ae3778c41c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049362 :topic/id #uuid "eefa1995-be29-5c63-a8ec-6c5199131e6e" :topic/math-academy-id 337} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065250 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065365 :tutorial/content "The average rate of change of the function $y=f(x)$ on the interval $[a,a+h]$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta x} = \\dfrac{f(a+h) - f(a)}{h}\n$$\n\nThis can be interpreted as the slope of the secant line that intersects the curve at the points $\\color{blue}P$ and ${\\color{red}Q}$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/32/3264f0792cee452453b024a61fb6f58a45290e133d3f84ec1ecf1c14501b4423.png)\n\nThe **instantaneous rate of change** of the function $f(x)$ at the point $x = a$ is the limit of the average rate of change as $h \\to 0$. It is denoted by $f'(a)$ and given by\n\n$$\nf'(a) = \\lim_{h\\to 0}\\dfrac {f(a+h) - f(a)} {h}\n$$\n\nGeometrically, as $h \\to 0$, the point $\\color{red}Q$ moves along the curve $y=f(x)$ towards the point ${\\color{blue}P}$, and the secant transforms into the line that touches the curve at exactly one point $({\\color{blue}P})$. That line is called the **tangent line** to the curve $y=f(x)$ at the point $\\color{blue}P$.\n\n![](images/37/3704b7e4f7a7aec6245d3494ef1e59a2b863e74a50bf5c9ecf471eec3789e7e9.png)\n\nThe value of $f'(a)$ is equal to the slope of the tangent line to the curve $y=f(x)$ at $x=a$." :tutorial/id #uuid "1f6bb245-1f46-5ade-84d4-6fd172b4b227" :tutorial/math-academy-id 322 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "35e9e9bc-7d8d-5422-a6d3-4890eb3c5548" :step/math-academy-id 595 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065251}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065251 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064337 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063933 :question/id #uuid "7f0eeea0-e493-5287-9633-8128758d5d57" :question/math-academy-id "e-125"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f8f2e684-6cd8-5ede-9f70-1a24ada54cb9" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048156} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049317} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049927}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064795} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064796} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233547} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235414}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Instantaneous Rate of Change of a Function at a Point"} :step/id #uuid "28874baa-b4bb-54af-b29b-a7cbfc639bc9" :step/math-academy-id 596 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065252}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065252 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065366 :tutorial/content "Another way of defining $f'(a)$ is\n\n$$\nf'(a) = \\lim_{x\\to a}\\dfrac {f(x) - f(a)} {x-a}\n$$\n\nWe arrive at this definition in the same way as before. But this time, we consider the interval $[a,x]$ instead of $[a,a+h]$, and then take the limit of the average rate of change as $x\\to a$.\n\n![](images/b4/b49c6bba1cb82227e7f47b2d1b91eae85ece94fd5251b79173842179cf7946e0.png)\n\nSometimes, this version is a bit easier to work with, so let's see an example of how to apply it. We should always arrive at the same answer regardless of which definition we use." :tutorial/id #uuid "6731f995-584b-5424-a320-c41c218307f9" :tutorial/math-academy-id 324 :tutorial/title "An Equivalent Definition of Instantaneous Rate of Change"} :step/id #uuid "2fde7718-a29e-5055-884f-3f58ca08aaec" :step/math-academy-id 599 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065253}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065253 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064338 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063934 :question/id #uuid "95edcdc3-a675-5963-9e1b-e9eb0595327d" :question/math-academy-id "e-126"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0ce0d18d-f1ee-5654-984d-f191abc9a410" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064794} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064797} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233067}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Slope of the Tangent Line to a Curve at a Point Using the Equivalent Definition"} :step/id #uuid "af0ad9db-1ba6-5216-8e5f-5d768427a09e" :step/math-academy-id 600}] :activity/title "The Instantaneous Rate of Change of a Function at a Point" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186064428}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106598} :activity/id #uuid "f5bc6246-bbe1-5e71-a301-e64564d76448" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049386 :topic/id #uuid "f0b09481-01c6-54c3-9c3a-c58994bdbc06" :topic/math-academy-id 1281} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106598 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107150 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we wish to find the area $\\mathcal{A}$ under the curve $y=x^2$ between the $x$-values $x=1$ and $x=4$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/35/3538fb94de9aac062e7ed65b9496085d478be911e5da2bf8480b260cec70ae4f.png)\n\nWe can approximate the area using, for instance, the area of three rectangles, each with a width equal to $1$.\n\n![](images/cb/cbb7b5af27aa5ebd3b5bbb51026017454999682dcf7e172411df79278564a5ae.png)\n\nSo, we find the area of each rectangle and then add them all together.\n\n$$\nA \\mid \\approx [f(2)^(⏞)^(height) \\cdot 1_(⏟)_(base)]^(⏞)^([math]1[/math]st rectangle) + [f(3)^(⏞)^(height) \\cdot 1_(⏟)_(base)]^(⏞)^([math]2[/math]nd rectangle) + [f(4)^(⏞)^(height) \\cdot 1_(⏟)_(base)]^(⏞)^([math]3[/math]rd rectangle); = [2^{2} \\cdot 1] + [3^{2} \\cdot 1] + [4^{2} \\cdot 1]; = 4 + 9 + 16; = 29\n$$\n\nHere, we approximated the area using a **right Riemann sum**, which means that the top-*right* corner of each rectangle touches the curve.\n\nBecause the function is *strictly increasing* over the interval $[1,4]$, the rectangles reach *above* the function, which means our approximation is an *overestimate* of the actual area (see the picture above)." :tutorial/id #uuid "a8dc0c69-e0be-5d9c-bb2a-4838709fb9eb" :tutorial/math-academy-id 733 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "39a0db46-10ca-5ca8-80cd-7262b7bef7bb" :step/math-academy-id 1411 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106600}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106599 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105721 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105577 :question/id #uuid "2eb23241-bd2b-5b76-b885-25cd3a51c16d" :question/math-academy-id "e-392"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f0efa50-9154-5cf3-bf01-e1b6eead489d" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048652} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049608}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106349} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106485} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233551} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235418}] :knowledge-point/title "Using the Right Riemann Sum With Regular Step Size"} :step/id #uuid "5ad9803e-7cb4-50f5-8c8e-7a9260fa6ed5" :step/math-academy-id 1412 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106753}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106600 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107151 :tutorial/content "In general, the area $\\mathcal{A}$ under the curve $y=f(x)$ over the interval $[a,b]$ can be approximated using a right Riemann sum by calculating the area of each rectangle and adding them together.\n\n![](images/6a/6aa3fd6dca84719aa7d34fdf75586a33005dd34c416d4456662f9d1876679f61.png)\n\nWe can write this mathematically as\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{A} \\approx f(x_1)\\Delta x+f(x_2)\\Delta x+f(x_3)\\Delta x+ \\cdots + f(x_n) \\Delta x\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is the number of rectangles and the step size $\\Delta x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\Delta x=\\dfrac{b-a}{n}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify by factoring out the $\\Delta x$, which gives the following general formula:\n\n$$\nheights\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "09dcf1d5-eddf-58d0-8f4b-eeae9cf26024" :tutorial/math-academy-id 735 :tutorial/title "A General Formula for the Right Riemann Sum"} :step/id #uuid "4b4ff77f-3654-5883-84bd-bc2a0712cbe5" :step/math-academy-id 1415 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106599}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106601 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107152 :tutorial/content "In the last few examples, the step size $\\Delta{x}$ was the same for each of the rectangles. In these cases, we say that the step size is **regular**.\n\nThere are situations where the step size can vary, which we call an **irregular step size**. But we can still use a right Riemann sum to approximate the area.\n\nFor example, let's use a right Riemann sum to approximate the area under the curve of the continuous function $y=f(x)$ whose data is given in the table below.\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $1.5$ | $2.5$ | $4$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $2$ | $6$ | $8$ |\n\nThe first thing we note is that the domain of the function $y=f(x)$ is *partitioned* as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} [1,4] &= [1, 1.5] \\cup [1.5, 2.5] \\cup [2.5, 4]. \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's plot what this function might look like, using the values given in the table.\n\n![](images/60/60c0bfba52fe087b4d8aaeceee367798e24079dc385afff3e83bca60405d4d2b.png)\n\nWe've drawn some rectangles to help us calculate our right Riemann sum, and we have chosen the heights of each rectangle to be calculated using the right endpoint of each interval.\n\nBy calculating the area of each rectangle, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n1\n$$\n\nPlugging our numbers into the above, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} \\mathcal{A} &\\approx {\\color{red}2} \\cdot ({\\color{blue}1.5-1}) + {\\color{red}6} \\cdot ({\\color{blue}2.5-1.5}) + {\\color{red}8} \\cdot ({\\color{blue}4-2.5}) \\\\ &= {\\color{red}2} \\cdot {\\color{blue}0.5} +{\\color{red}6} \\cdot {\\color{blue}1}+ {\\color{red}8} \\cdot {\\color{blue}1.5} \\\\ &=1+6+12 \\\\ &= 19. \\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "b662a09b-80ce-5d1d-8950-1d407892ead3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 736 :tutorial/title "Right Riemann Sums with Irregular Step Size"} :step/id #uuid "7d5f671d-e0d4-5b0a-8317-1304ba7d929f" :step/math-academy-id 1416 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106602}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106602 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105722 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105578 :question/id #uuid "41e80d7f-8a72-5bbf-9f66-c0047b28e45b" :question/math-academy-id "e-394"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ecb9df79-5227-5fc6-9a20-818fee34e971" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106367} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106368} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234315} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287984} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319104} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342196} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186372981}] :knowledge-point/title "Using the Right Riemann Sum With Irregular Step Size"} :step/id #uuid "e15fac05-66ad-5f49-9bed-51db8cc7f71c" :step/math-academy-id 1420} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106753 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105814 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105648 :question/id #uuid "c1deb898-8696-537f-8fb2-ae0f354dec29" :question/math-academy-id "e-7231"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "01fe05cd-8acc-5676-9328-7a26362a77d0" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106364} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106486} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250475} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250476} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319102} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354467}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Given the Value of a Right Riemann Sum With Regular Step Size"} :step/id #uuid "f11aa5d6-b2b3-51c0-8bd1-0228ad7b133b" :step/math-academy-id 26749 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106754}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106754 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107100 :tutorial/content "Whether the right Riemann sum underestimates or overestimates the actual area under a function depends on whether the function is increasing or decreasing.\n\n- If the function $f(x)$ is increasing, then the rectangles reach *above* the function, which means the right Riemann sum gives an *overestimate* of the actual area under the curve $y=f(x)$.![](images/71/71bc3147a2d7ee5867740e67f55d2a257065f0f225b62b4af3b5f5990c0d4ffc.png)\n- If the function $f(x)$ is decreasing, then the rectangles fall *below* the function, which means the right Riemann sum gives an *underestimate* of the actual area under the curve $y=f(x)$.\n\n![](images/0b/0bc5bc2d5daa2b42e640d31b998707af503b4c4489531ef7ddd41f4374405ef7.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "ca191bef-f207-5cb0-88a1-6c46288c5c09" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4373 :tutorial/title "Overestimates and Underestimates"} :step/id #uuid "efefd8b4-c7ee-50d3-abef-483a7d25557b" :step/math-academy-id 26751 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106755}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106755 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105815 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105649 :question/id #uuid "1f74cbf1-c3f5-5ac2-ad55-ad22d7dae3ca" :question/math-academy-id "e-7233"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad9b9344-1711-5ff4-b489-f6ef2a0bd2eb" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049573}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106369} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106370} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250468} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319106} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386778} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386785}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Right Riemann Sum is an Overestimate or Underestimate"} :step/id #uuid "a49d1998-d787-5d44-a81a-b54f11630c6b" :step/math-academy-id 26752 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106601}}] :activity/title "Approximating Areas With the Right Riemann Sum" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105959}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106812} :activity/id #uuid "fbe08d91-7f85-5aca-90e3-9537e43e9705" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049637 :topic/id #uuid "072aa5e8-f5da-53f2-8406-4aa8a1bc1e24" :topic/math-academy-id 1263} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106560 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107064 :tutorial/content "Constants do not vary with $x.$ So, any limit of a constant evaluates to just the constant itself.\n\nFor example, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty} 3=3.\n$$\n\nLikewise, \n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to -\\infty} \\sqrt{ \\pi}= \\sqrt{\\pi} .\n$$\n\nIn general, for any constant $C,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\pm\\infty} C=C.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "19052219-09a5-5548-a770-353adefe214a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2581 :tutorial/title "Infinite Limits of Constant Functions"} :step/id #uuid "45bc2482-5b40-5a90-b961-e785160dde91" :step/math-academy-id 12015 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106561}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106561 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105700 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105563 :question/id #uuid "3977d627-65bc-5f36-8c25-7d47b515f383" :question/math-academy-id "e-3566"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6456693-8845-5f7b-8b0b-2d6802bbf72e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106287} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106466} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233871} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234238} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297261} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297262} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297267} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311770} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311775} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335934} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348062}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit at Infinity of a Constant Function"} :step/id #uuid "96ffef5f-bd71-5fb3-8b87-d6ba58b7e210" :step/math-academy-id 12016 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106827}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106747 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107030 :tutorial/content "Multiplication of a power function by any *positive* real constant does not influence its limit at infinity. For example,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }2{x}^{2} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{2}=\\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbecause $y=2x^2$ is an upward parabola, just like $y=x^2.$\n\n![](images/47/47d8cef2d3d189b32ee1c37f9fa563f58670d0bdc876360b13753106df03a79d.png)\n\nOn the other hand, multiplication of a power function by a *negative* real constant changes the *sign* of the limit. For example,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }-2{x}^{2}=-{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{2}=-\\infty \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nbecause $y=-2x^2$ is a downwards parabola that points in the opposite direction as $y=x^2.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "6ae2ce56-a932-54d8-a6e9-9eea329c623b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1335 :tutorial/title "The Effect of Coefficients"} :step/id #uuid "17d3a58d-07af-5d54-aca7-37b0e9e2f9b5" :step/math-academy-id 2627 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106813}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106812 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107038 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to calculate $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty} x^2$ and $\\lim_\\limits{x \\to -\\infty} x^2.$ To do this, we can use the graph of $y=x^2,$ which takes the familiar shape of a parabola.\n\n![](images/58/58536c20f942b37e75a1ebb60fa6298af5fa94d1ba7b2b1cd770a0251796b436.png)\n\nOn the right side of the graph, we see that $y$ increases without bound as $x \\to \\infty.$ On the left side of the graph, we have a similar situation: $y$ again increases without bound as $x \\to -\\infty.$ Consequently, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty} x^2=\\infty\\qquad \\textrm{and}\\qquad \\lim_\\limits{x \\to -\\infty} x^2=\\infty.\n$$\n\nNotice that the functions $y=x^4$ and $y=x^6$ have a similar shape, and as such their limiting behavior is the same.\n\n![](images/5e/5e7386db15731b3486fd64484db69e7919c8e79821d916974f689d737cf23f8a.png)\n\nIn general, whenever $n$ is an *even natural number*, we have\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty} x^n=\\infty\\qquad \\textrm{and}\\qquad \\lim_\\limits{x \\to -\\infty} x^n=\\infty, \\qquad \\textrm{for}\\qquad n=2,4,6,\\ldots.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "9981abcd-17f0-549f-9dcc-067b9bc17a0f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 190 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "6a8c98da-eef2-5791-8a0c-e4700a11a8d6" :step/math-academy-id 353 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106747}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106813 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105849 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105564 :question/id #uuid "88c456d9-e5d3-5b33-a847-03f201751c19" :question/math-academy-id "e-36"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4974097a-95c9-5835-ba1d-46480efab66b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050046} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050259}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106286} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106289} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233758} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234508} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297264} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297265} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297266} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320986} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362355}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit at Infinity of a Power Function With an Even Power"} :step/id #uuid "096e64b1-f521-5ed3-8f48-cd30efc9ff5e" :step/math-academy-id 354 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106818}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106818 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107039 :tutorial/content "Now let's look at the graph of $y=x^3.$\n\n![](images/53/53d67c89fce03c6b949aecbe1e55fe8bf912ebadf1c3edc6d6c3601047886b5a.png)\n\nWe notice that the situation here is different:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\to \\infty} x^3=\\infty \\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad \\lim_{x \\to -\\infty} x^3=-\\infty.\n$$\n\nThese limits are valid for any power function of the form $x^n,$ where $n$ is an *odd natural number*.\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\to \\infty} x^n=\\infty\\qquad \\textrm{and}\\qquad \\lim_\\limits{x \\to -\\infty} x^n=-\\infty, \\qquad n=1,3,5,\\ldots.\n$$\n\nAgain, multiplication of $x^n$ by a positive constant does not affect the limit at infinity. However, multiplication by a negative constant changes the sign of the limit." :tutorial/id #uuid "d9d4d60e-df4b-5dbb-85e6-e53ab24b16f2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 192 :tutorial/title "Computing the Limit at Infinity of a Power Function With an Odd Power"} :step/id #uuid "c2cbae50-d421-561d-b4fe-f6dcc8a8591d" :step/math-academy-id 357 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106820}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106820 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105854 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105565 :question/id #uuid "551f2aa0-8589-5725-abe6-de1b7eca0327" :question/math-academy-id "e-37"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5bea806-2913-57f5-ba32-be7aeda5bd15" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106285} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106288} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233761} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233868} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233997} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297260} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311764} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311765} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320929}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit at Infinity of a Power Function With an Odd Power"} :step/id #uuid "800c9745-b1c3-57bb-8946-69f8df02ae03" :step/math-academy-id 358 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106560}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106827 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186107040 :tutorial/content "To compute the limit at infinity of a general polynomial, we *only* need to consider the term with the largest exponent, called the **leading term**. All of the other terms can be disregarded!\n\nFor example, to compute \n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\to \\infty} \\left( 6x + 2x^3-7x^2+12\\right) \\, ,\n$$\n\n we identify the leading term and compute its limit. Here, the leading term is ${\\color{blue}{2x^3}},$ so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(6x+2{x}^{3}-7{x}^{2}+12) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(2{x}^{3}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({x}^{3}) \\\\  & =\\infty \\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reason this works is that when $x$ becomes very large, the behavior of the polynomial is dominated by the term with the largest exponent.\n\n**Watch out!** The leading term is *not* always the first term that appears in the expression. Rather, it is the term *with the largest exponent*. This term may appear anywhere in the expression.\n\nThe reason why we call the term with the largest exponent the \"leading\" term is that when the polynomial is arranged in standard form (with exponents ordered from greatest to least), the term with the largest exponent appears first:\n\n$$\n6x+ {\\color{blue}{2x^3}}-7x^2+12 \\quad \\to \\quad {\\color{blue}{2x^3}}-7x^2+6x+12\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "549fd7b8-2367-5c38-b26e-475d7c827a1e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 196 :tutorial/title "Limits at Infinity of General Polynomials"} :step/id #uuid "5e48e500-3385-5631-9674-a6c6b177bfcd" :step/math-academy-id 363 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106828}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106828 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105857 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105576 :question/id #uuid "969d5f07-c5ce-5601-b2de-0d23eb15809b" :question/math-academy-id "e-39"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc04f720-28f3-55ab-8e89-582db57672a4" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106303} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186106452} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233545} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233759} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234000} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235412} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311782} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311783} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311784} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311785} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311786} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320803} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362357} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402402} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402675}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Limit at Infinity of a General Polynomial"} :step/id #uuid "923659ef-18ec-5fc8-8fb8-ff70ca00d5c0" :step/math-academy-id 364}] :activity/title "Limits at Infinity of Polynomials" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186105950}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 720.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079469} :activity/id #uuid "da39b17e-570c-50b3-ac5f-dba85525f10f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050225 :topic/id #uuid "3797cf55-eabb-554f-a0af-7378ce7b2ec9" :topic/math-academy-id 604} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079469 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079646 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we want to calculate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x \\to 1} \\frac{\\sqrt{x} - 1}{x - 1}\n$$\n\nIf we try to evaluate the limit by directly substituting $x=1,$ we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 1}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-1}{x-1} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{1}-1}{1-1}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, if we simplify the fraction before evaluating the limit, we obtain a more clear result. To simplify the fraction, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator of the function by the **conjugate** of the numerator.\n\nTo obtain the conjugate of $\\sqrt{x}-1,$ we simply switch the sign between the two terms to get $\\sqrt{x}+ 1.$ \n\n$$\n\\eqalign{\n\\require{cancel}\n\\lim_{x\\to 1} \\frac{\\sqrt{x} - 1} {x - 1}\n\t&= \\lim_{x\\to 1} \\frac{\\sqrt{x} - 1} {x - 1} \\cdot\\dfrac{\\sqrt{x} + 1}{\\sqrt{x} + 1}\t\\\n\t&=\\lim_{x\\to 1} \\dfrac{(\\sqrt{x} - 1)(\\sqrt{x} +1)} {(x-1)(\\sqrt{x} + 1)}\\\n        &=\\lim_{x\\to 1} \\dfrac{\\left(\\sqrt{x}\\right)^2 - 1^2} {(x-1)(\\sqrt{x} + 1)}\\\n\t&=\\lim_{x\\to 1} \\dfrac{x-1} {(x-1)(\\sqrt{x} + 1)}\\\n\t&=\\lim_{x\\to 1} \\dfrac{\\cancel{x-1}} {\\cancel{(x-1)}(\\sqrt{x} + 1)}\\\n\t&=\\lim_{x\\to 1} \\dfrac1{\\sqrt{x} + 1}\\\n        &=\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{1} +1}\\\n\t&=\\dfrac{1}{2}\n}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "73481739-fdce-5a28-84e1-5145ea62af87" :tutorial/math-academy-id 250 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "d20c032d-424d-59b2-947c-bd6f83dc0b44" :step/math-academy-id 453 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079470}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079470 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078536 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078358 :question/id #uuid "06d5e05a-4595-551f-8c1b-7c585f69b47b" :question/math-academy-id "e-74"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a441c8ef-b5b3-5438-97b9-53e4eb7b2d75" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048758} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049927}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078923} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078926} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233047} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233048} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233093} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233546} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233883} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233884} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233885} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234058} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235413} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244012} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297273} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311948} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311949} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311950} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329759} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329796} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329797} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329798} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332842} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363692} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186363953} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364199} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364282}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Limit of a Rational Function with a Radical in the Numerator"} :step/id #uuid "e7f2241e-1a39-5fd2-bf4c-e20365acd894" :step/math-academy-id 454 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079500}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079500 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078557 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078359 :question/id #uuid "7db311cd-70a0-50f4-8984-4a578abc67eb" :question/math-academy-id "e-75"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "045722b6-b558-522b-86e1-322c64e2848f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078924} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078953} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233049} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233886} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234063} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234064} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234065} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237815} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244013} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297274} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297275} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311952} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311953} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311954} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186311958} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329758} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329761} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329795} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186362630} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364206} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364289}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Limit of a Rational Function with a Radical in the Denominator"} :step/id #uuid "93db8b23-0bbc-5091-a87e-379d4484afba" :step/math-academy-id 458}] :activity/title "Calculating Limits of Radical Functions Using Conjugate Multiplication" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186078635}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125558} :activity/id #uuid "5be1aba9-8d05-5cc5-8d16-2faeb26e75c0" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050653 :topic/id #uuid "5cd64b32-8cbc-5b0f-b4b4-7b7dad877ee2" :topic/math-academy-id 1914} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125316 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124468 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124272 :question/id #uuid "4cbd707a-c3a7-5f68-803f-15941e2fd4b9" :question/math-academy-id "e-3560"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dad9bdc2-a243-5437-8904-9906c2d9a90e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050304}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124864} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125159} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233548} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233550} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235415} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235417}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Sum Rule to Compute a Limit"} :step/id #uuid "2c0eea00-4c79-58bd-a141-08872cc00730" :step/math-academy-id 12000 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125561}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125558 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125718 :tutorial/content "The **sum rule** states that the limit of the sum of two functions equals the sum of the limits (provided that those limits exist).\n\nMore precisely, if\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow a}f(x)=L\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow a}g(x)=K\n$$\n\n, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → a)(f(x) + g(x)) &= lim_(x → a)f(x) + lim_(x → a)g(x) \\\\\n&= L + K\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor example, to compute\n\n$$\n\\lim_\\limits{x \\rightarrow \\,-3} \\left(5x^2+2x\\right)\n$$\n\nwe can first apply the sum rule, followed by the constant rule, and compute the limits of the power functions.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nlim_(x → - 3)(5x^{2} + 2x) &= lim_(x → - 3)5x^{2} + lim_(x → - 3)2x \\\\\n&= 5 \\cdot lim_(x → - 3)x^{2} + 2 \\cdot lim_(x → - 3)x \\\\\n&= 5 \\cdot (-3)^{2} + 2 \\cdot (-3) \\\\\n&= 45 - 6 \\\\\n&= 39\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere is a shortcut: in general, to find the limit of a polynomial $f(x)$ at some point $x=c$, all we need to do is evaluate the polynomial at $c$.\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to c} f(x) = f(c)\n$$\n\n**Warning!** If one of $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow a}f(x)$ or $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow a}g(x)$ exists while the other does not exist, then $\\lim_\\limits{x\\rightarrow a}\\Bigl(f(x)+g(x) \\Bigr)$ does not exist either." :tutorial/id #uuid "6a3580c6-229d-54ff-b09f-3bc257eadf2f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 171 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "de7a6cde-8613-5323-be0d-287774012b07" :step/math-academy-id 324 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125316}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125561 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124613 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124238 :question/id #uuid "d1efe60f-bf85-56a4-a711-778a4c2f4003" :question/math-academy-id "e-25"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "38e6d549-0d5a-56e3-88b0-0b22dceb2d0d" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124834} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125173} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233390} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234522} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235379}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Sum Rule to Compute A Limit Given a Graph"} :step/id #uuid "f4817a4a-39ad-58fc-80ec-554cf0c34b53" :step/math-academy-id 330}] :activity/title "The Sum Rule for Limits" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124713}]}
